---

Appwrite is moving its server from PHP to Rust, one piece at a time. Appwrite has run on PHP since its first release in 2019, and the first piece of its Rust future is already live.

Since November 12, every image preview and avatar on Appwrite Cloud has been rendered by Rust. In our measurements, the slowest previews took about a third of the time, previews returned no server errors for three days, and no app had to change.

# Why we are moving to Rust

Appwrite's job is to be the fast, reliable part of your stack, so that your team can spend its time on the product. We expect Rust to take Appwrite further on both as more of the server moves.

- **Speed where it counts:** in our image-processing benchmarks, CPU-heavy work such as decoding and resizing ran several times faster.
- **Less memory per request:** in early tests, the Rust Storage service peaked at about a third of the PHP service's memory, leaving more room for traffic.
- **Fewer moving parts:** Rust code replaces external native tools such as ImageMagick, along with the updates they require.

# How the move works

A full rewrite in one go would compete with everything else we ship. We are moving one piece at a time instead, while Appwrite keeps shipping.

- **Libraries first:** each library is rebuilt in Rust and loaded into today's PHP server under the same class names. It runs against the PHP library's own tests and a corpus that compares both versions, then rolls out behind a switch, with PHP one restart away.
- **Then whole services:** most of a request waits on the database and the network, which a single Rust library cannot speed up, so the larger gains need a whole service on Rust. Users, Teams, Account, and Storage already run on Rust in development, checked against the PHP services' end-to-end tests.

# Faster image previews on Appwrite Cloud
{% only_dark %}
![Image preview response times on Appwrite Cloud before and after Rust](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/latency-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![Image preview response times on Appwrite Cloud before and after Rust](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/latency.avif)
{% /only_light %}

We compared three days on PHP with three days on Rust in our Frankfurt region. Previews that are not cached are the only ones that run the image engine, and they are the ones that got faster.

- **Median:** **390–530 ms** to **260–285 ms**.
- **p95, the time 95% of requests finish within:** **1.0–2.4 s** to about **0.65 s**.
- **p99, the time 99% of requests finish within:** **3.4–4.3 s** to **1.1–1.5 s**.
- **Initials avatars:** median **92 ms** to **32 ms**, and p95 **230 ms** to **58 ms**.

# More traffic and no preview server errors
{% only_dark %}
![Daily preview requests and server errors on Appwrite Cloud before and after Rust](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/errors-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![Daily preview requests and server errors on Appwrite Cloud before and after Rust](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/errors.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Preview traffic grew from **1.3 million** to **2.3–2.9 million** requests a day in the same week. During the three days measured on Rust, previews returned no server errors, down from **46–57** a day on PHP.

- **CPU:** up about **12%** while handling about **60%** more traffic. Memory stayed flat.
- **Cached previews:** more than **98%** of requests reuse a stored preview and stay at about **35–40 ms**, since they skip image processing entirely.
- **A caveat on the comparison:** one project started requesting far more small thumbnails that week, so the traffic mix changed between the two periods. For that project alone, the p95 of uncached thumbnails went from **931 ms** to **449 ms**.

# How we shipped it safely
{% only_dark %}
![The rollout of Rust image processing, from a canary region to every Appwrite Cloud region](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/rollout-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![The rollout of Rust image processing, from a canary region to every Appwrite Cloud region](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/rollout.avif)
{% /only_light %}

The Rust engine went live in our New York region first, and in all **six** Cloud regions the next day. PHP stayed one restart away the whole time. Once Rust had served every region, we deleted the PHP version, about **1,350** lines, and removed ImageMagick from the Appwrite image.

Before any of that, both engines processed the same **1,124** test images, valid and damaged. The comparison found **three** bugs, all fixed before rollout. One was in the PHP version running in production: some photos with a rotation tag came out rotated twice.

# What already runs on Rust
{% only_dark %}
![Appwrite libraries that run on Rust on Appwrite Cloud and on the conversion branch](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/libraries-dark.avif)
{% /only_dark %}

{% only_light %}
![Appwrite libraries that run on Rust on Appwrite Cloud and on the conversion branch](/images/blog/appwrite-is-moving-to-rust/libraries.avif)
{% /only_light %}

Image processing was the first library to move, not the last. **Six** more now serve every Appwrite Cloud region from Rust, and their PHP versions are gone. On the conversion branch, about **50** of Appwrite's libraries already have Rust versions.

- **Domain parsing:** in benchmarks, the first domain name a process checks went from **17 ms** to **1.8 ms**.
- **Email checks:** checking whether an address belongs to a free email provider went from **492 µs** to **1.3 µs**.
- **And more:** connection strings for databases and caches, translations, user agent parsing (identifying the browser or app behind a request), and the validators that check every request's input.
- **Swoole:** the runtime that handles concurrent requests in Appwrite's PHP server now builds from swoole-rs, our Rust rewrite of Swoole.

Not every library got faster on its own. Thin libraries that mostly call back into PHP are tens of nanoseconds slower, which is exactly why the rest of Appwrite moves as whole services.

# Early numbers from Storage on Rust

The Storage service already runs on Rust in development and passes the same **108** end-to-end tests, with **2,883** assertions, as the PHP service. These numbers come from a busy development machine, not production, so treat them as a direction rather than a promise.

- **At 50 requests a second:** p99 went from **721 ms** to **328 ms**, CPU per request from **36.5 ms** to **12.6 ms**, and peak memory from **2.6 GB** to **0.9 GB**.
- **At 50 concurrent users:** Rust served **487** requests a second, against **209** for PHP.
- **At 200 requests a second:** Rust kept up with a median of **6.5 ms**, while PHP managed only **106**.

# What changes for your app

The API, SDKs, and Console stay the same, and each piece is checked against the same tests before it reaches Appwrite Cloud, so no migration is needed. How much faster your app gets depends on the work it asks Appwrite to do.

# Building the next Appwrite

Moving Appwrite to Rust is the biggest engineering change in the project's history, and we are doing it in the open, one measured step at a time. We will keep sharing the numbers as more of Appwrite moves over.

We would love to hear what you would like to see move to Rust next. Come and tell us on [Discord](https://appwrite.io/discord).

